# Part D — Extensions

## 11. Light as boundary — the causal-structure reading of c

Independently, in conversations that never mention Lumen (developed with Gregor), the case was built that $c$ is better read as an invariant feature of causal geometry than "how fast light happens to move," and that light "exists everywhere it can exist all at once" rather than traveling as an object. That is, structurally, what Part A §2 already states: Light *is* the boundary and the coupling force, not a traveling thing. Lumen is close to a formal home for that intuition, arrived at independently of it.

## 12. Cosmological extension — local Burden and global contraction

**[PROPOSED] — hypothesis, not a derived result.**

Independent cosmology work (this project) builds a detailed case for a directional, anisotropic cosmic recollapse: vector-field dark energy rolling to negative energy (AdS transition), BKL/mixmaster instability amplifying seed anisotropy during contraction, consistent with DESI's time-varying, possibly directional dark-energy signature.

The vector-field AdS-collapse scenario has no ekpyrotic ($w>1$) phase — nothing available to smooth or absorb the growing directional shear. Using §7–§8's vocabulary: accumulation with no available consolidation mechanism. Burden has nowhere to be exported to, so it saturates globally rather than being relegated locally. The chaotic mixmaster crunch is what failed closure (§8) looks like at cosmological scale. A successful bounce needs exactly a stiff phase — structurally, a consolidation mechanism operating globally, which the vector-field model doesn't supply.

Specific enough to be wrong in a useful way if it's wrong. Not yet checked against the formal apparatus of Part B or C.

